WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.450
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.129
When an artist is in the process of creating
digital media, such as populating a virtual

00:00:09.129 --> 00:00:14.809
world for an animation movie or a video game,
or even in graphic design, the artist often

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:18.820
requires a large number of textures for these
kinds of works.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:23.680
Concrete walls, leaves, fabrics are materials
that we know well from the real world and

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.910
sometimes the process of obtaining textures
is as simple as paying for a texture package

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.020
and using it.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:35.149
But the problem quite often occurs that we
wish to fill an entire road with a concrete

00:00:35.149 --> 00:00:38.390
texture, but we only have a small patch at
our disposal.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:43.719
In this case, the easiest and worst solution
is to copy-paste this texture over and over,

00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:48.359
creating really unpleasant results that are
quite repetitive and suffer from seams.

00:00:48.359 --> 00:00:53.430
So what about an AI-based technique that looks
at a small patch, and automatically continues

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.019
it in a way that looks natural and seamless.

00:00:57.019 --> 00:01:01.800
This is an area within computer graphics and
AI that we call texture synthesis.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.090
Periodic texture synthesis is simple, but
textures with structure are super difficult.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:11.250
The selling point of this particular work
is that it is highly efficient at taking into

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.850
consideration the content and symmetries of
the image.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.350
For instance, it knows that it has to take
into consideration the concentric nature of

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:24.259
the wood rings when synthesizing this texture,
and it can also adapt to the regularities

00:01:24.259 --> 00:01:28.920
of this water texture and create a beautiful,
high-resolution result.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.780
This is a neural-network based technique,
so first, the question is, what should the

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.250
training data be?

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.200
Let's take a database of high-resolution images.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.340
Let's cut out a small part and pretend that
we don't have access to the bigger image and

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.570
ask a neural network to try to expand this
small cutout.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:50.009
This sounds a little silly, so what is this
trickery good for?

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:54.850
Well, this is super useful because after the
neural network has expanded the results, we

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:59.670
now have a reference result in our hands that
we can compare to and this way, teach the

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.329
network to do better.

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:05.979
Note that this architecture is a generative
adversarial network, where two neural networks

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:07.299
battle each other.

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:12.260
The generator network is the creator that
expands the small texture snippets, and the

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.180
discriminator network takes a look and tries
to tell it from the real deal.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:21.470
Over time, the generator network learns to
be better at texture synthesis, and the discriminator

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:25.459
network becomes better at telling synthesized
results from real ones.

00:02:25.459 --> 00:02:30.450
Over time, this rivalry leads to results that
are of extremely high quality.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.940
And as you can see in this comparison, this
new technique smokes the competition.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:39.459
The paper contains a ton of more results and
comparisons, and one of the most exhaustive

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:43.050
evaluation sections I've seen in texture synthesis
so far.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.900
I highly recommend reading it.

